  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "A point is randomly chosen inside of a unit square (uniformly). Find the probability that the point is less than 1 unit away from all four vertices of the square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p40-q20-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "1+\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt{3}",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p40-q20-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Find the area common to the four unit-radius quarter circles; the unit square has area 1.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "The required region is the intersection of four quarter circles of radius 1, centred at the square's vertices. Since the square has area 1, its area equals the required probability.\n\nTake the square's vertices as $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(1,1)$ and $(0,1)$. The four boundary junctions are $(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2})$, $(\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\tfrac12)$, $(\\tfrac12,1-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2})$ and $(1-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\tfrac12)$. Joining them forms a square with both diagonals of length $\\sqrt3-1$. Each of the four remaining circular segments subtends $\\tfrac{\\pi}{6}$ at its centre.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{inner square}}&=\\frac12(\\sqrt3-1)^2\\\\A_{\\text{inner square}}&=2-\\sqrt3\\\\A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}-\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\\\A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac{\\pi}{12}-\\frac14\\\\P&=(2-\\sqrt3)+4\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}-\\frac14\\right)\\\\P&=1+\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt3\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
